\subsection{Phase F: constructive stochastic practical stability}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-f}

The stochastic theorem in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} intentionally does
not infer a Gaussian drift inequality from deterministic bounded-input ISS.  A
samplewise converse metric $P_k$ is also not the right object for this step when
innovation acceptance is random, because a metric constructed from a complete
future switching sequence need not be measurable at the current filtration.
Phase F therefore lifts the proof to the finite contraction window of Phase B
and uses the ordinary Euclidean norm at those lifted indices.  The resulting
Lyapunov function is common to every admissible accepted/rejected branch and is
nonanticipative.

Fix a source mode of constant active dimension and the Phase-B window length
$N$ for which $\kappa_N<1$.  Let $k_n=k_0+nN$.  For a realized length-$N$
source branch $\beta_n$ (including accepted/rejected updates and any parameter
switching allowed by $\Pi^{\rm env}$), let
$\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}$ denote the zero-noise nonlinear lifted map obtained by
holding that same branch sequence fixed while setting the sensor/process
increments to zero.  Its linearization at the origin is
$\Psi_{n,\beta_n}$ and Phase B gives
\begin{equation}
 \|\Psi_{n,\beta_n}\|\le\kappa_N<1
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-map}
\end{equation}
for every admissible branch.  On a compact Phase-C tube of Euclidean radius
$r_{\mathcal T}$ define the finite lifted nonlinear coefficient
\begin{equation}
 L_N:=
 \sup_{n,\beta_n}
 \sup_{\substack{0<\|z\|\le r_{\mathcal T}\\
                  \text{all zero-noise prefixes remain in }\mathcal T}}
 \frac{\|\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z)-
          \Psi_{n,\beta_n}z\|}{\|z\|^2}<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}
\end{equation}
Each fixed source branch is smooth on the compact geodesic/measurement family,
so the bound is finite.  Define the deterministic lifted contraction
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \kappa_c:=\kappa_N+L_N r_{\mathcal T}<1.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}
\end{equation}
Then, pointwise for every admissible branch,
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z)\|
 \le\kappa_c\|z\|.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise}
\end{equation}
This condition is stronger than mere local UES but is finite-window and
nonanticipative; it is the stochastic analogue of choosing a robust inner
Phase-C tube.

For one physical sample on a fixed realized branch, write the difference
between the noisy and branch-matched zero-noise step as $y_k$.  Uniform
smoothness on the stopped tube gives
\begin{equation}
 \|y_k\|
 \le \bar G\|w_k\|
 +c_{zw}r_{\mathcal T}\|w_k\|
 +c_{ww}\|w_k\|^2.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-one-step-noise}
\end{equation}
The branch is allowed to depend on $w_k$; no zero conditional mean after NIS
gating is assumed.  Assume only the pre-gate conditional moments
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\|w_k\|^2\mid\mathscr F_k]\le s_2,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E[\|w_k\|^4\mid\mathscr F_k]\le s_4.
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-noise}
\end{equation}
For conditionally Gaussian pre-gate noise with
$\Sigma_k\preceq\overline\Sigma$, these may be chosen as
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 s_2=\operatorname{tr}\overline\Sigma,
 \qquad
 s_4=s_2^2+2\operatorname{tr}(\overline\Sigma^2).}
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-moments}
\end{equation}
The theorem therefore uses Gaussianity only to evaluate the two moments; any
other noise family with the same uniform moment bounds is admissible.

Let $L_x<\infty$ be a uniform state-Lipschitz constant of one fixed-branch
source step on the stopped tube.  Define
\begin{align}
 g_{\mathcal T}&:=\bar G+c_{zw}r_{\mathcal T},\\
 \nu_1&:=2g_{\mathcal T}^2s_2+2c_{ww}^2s_4,\\
 \boxed{
 \nu_N:=N\nu_1\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}L_x^{2r}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-floor}
\end{align}
No independence between the $N$ physical samples is required.  Indeed, if
$Y_n$ is the difference between the complete noisy lifted map and its
branch-matched zero-noise map, repeated Lipschitz propagation and
Cauchy--Schwarz give
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\|Y_n\|^2\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 \le\nu_N.
 \label{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive lifted mean-square practical drift under gated noise]
\label{thm:widen-stochastic-drift}
Assume Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}--
\eqref{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}.  Let
\begin{equation}
 V_n:=\|z_{k_n}\|^2,
 \qquad
 \boxed{
 \lambda_s:=\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{2}<1,
 \qquad
 \sigma_s^2:=
 \frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{1-\kappa_c^2}\,\nu_N.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-coefficients}
\end{equation}
For the process stopped on exit from the certified Phase-C tube,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_{n+1}\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 \le\lambda_s V_n+\sigma_s^2.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
\end{equation}
Consequently the stochastic certificate condition of
Theorem~\ref{thm:capture-stochastic-live} holds on these lifted normal-Live
indices with explicitly constructed $(\lambda_s,\sigma_s^2)$, and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E V_n
 \le\lambda_s^{n-j}\mathbb E V_j
 +\frac{1-\lambda_s^{n-j}}{1-\lambda_s}\sigma_s^2.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the realized branch sequence write
$z_{k_{n+1}}=\mathcal F_{n,\beta_n}(z_{k_n})+Y_n$.  The branch itself may be a
function of the within-window noise; this causes no measurability problem
because Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise} holds pointwise for every
admissible branch.  For any $0<\kappa_c<1$, choose
\begin{equation}
 \varepsilon=\frac{1-\kappa_c^2}{2\kappa_c^2}>0.
\end{equation}
The deterministic inequality
$\|a+b\|^2\le(1+\varepsilon)\|a\|^2+
(1+\varepsilon^{-1})\|b\|^2$, followed by
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-zero-noise} and
\eqref{eq:widen-lifted-noise-moment}, gives
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E[V_{n+1}\mid\mathscr F_{k_n}]
 &\le(1+\varepsilon)\kappa_c^2V_n
 +(1+\varepsilon^{-1})\nu_N\\
 &=\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{2}V_n
 +\frac{1+\kappa_c^2}{1-\kappa_c^2}\nu_N,
\end{align}
which is Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}.  Iteration gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}.  The degenerate case $\kappa_c=0$ follows
by continuity or directly from the same inequality with any fixed
$\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

Because Gaussian noise is unbounded, a regional nonlinear theorem should not
silently claim almost-sure invariance of a compact tube.  Let
$V_{\partial}=r_{\partial}^2$ be a Euclidean sublevel contained in the certified
Phase-C tube.  A union bound followed by Markov's inequality and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms} gives, for $K$ lifted steps,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb P\!\left(
 \max_{0\le r\le K}V_{j+r}\ge V_{\partial}
 \right)
 \le
 \min\!\left\{1,
 \frac{\mathbb E V_j+(K+1)\sigma_s^2}
 {(1-\lambda_s)V_{\partial}}\right\}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-exit}
\end{equation}
This deliberately conservative finite-horizon bound separates the normal-Live
mean-square statement from rare excursions that invoke the source's rejection,
relock, and hybrid-recovery logic.

\begin{remark}[What Phase F closes]
\label{rem:widen-stochastic-scope}
Phase F removes the need to posit both stochastic drift coefficients
independently of the deterministic analysis: once the finite deterministic
contraction $\kappa_N$, the lifted nonlinear coefficient $L_N$, the stopped-tube
one-step Lipschitz/noise constants, and the declared second/fourth moments are
bounded, $(\lambda_s,\sigma_s^2)$ follow analytically.  The use of a common
$N$-step Euclidean contraction avoids an anticipative switching-dependent
Lyapunov metric and does not require a zero conditional mean.  It does not turn
a regional MEKF into a global Gaussian system, and it does not assume that NIS
gating preserves zero conditional mean.  Unbounded-noise excursions are
explicitly separated from the normal-Live mean-square result and are handled by
an exit probability plus the hybrid recovery theorem rather than being hidden
inside a deterministic bounded-input assumption.
\end{remark}
